msgctxt "seo_title"
msgid "Who Owns Your Digital Life"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "search_description"
msgid "As people push back against Big Tech and AI, a growing movement is fighting for data ownership, digital sovereignty and a more equitable internet built on consent."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "title"
msgid "Who Owns Your Digital Life?"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "hero_image_alt_text"
msgid "Image by Logan Voss"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "lede_text"
msgid ""
"As the massive popularity of Spotify Wrapped and similar features like Letterboxd’s ‘Year in Review’ has made clear, users are interested in accessing and parsing their data as a means of "
"quantifying and understanding themselves. However, this interest is colliding with a growing backlash to the AI boom and wariness about personal information being scraped for profit."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.24de4250-77cb-431e-ab21-0f6bd877f377"
msgid ""
"After Rob Cheung got a speeding ticket earlier this year, he ran into a problem: he could not remember the name of the small county in rural New York where he was cited in order to pay the fine."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.24de4250-77cb-431e-ab21-0f6bd877f377"
msgid ""
"Since co-founding personal cloud service Zo Computer in 2023, Cheung has been uploading vast swathes of his own personal data to the platform to create what he calls a centralized “Rob App.” Having "
"given the cloud access to his location data, he asked the AI assistant where he may have been—and it quickly told him the county he was likely in and which judge he could contact regarding the fine."
" <b>He said this experience underscored for him how the vast amounts of data people hand over to corporations could be better used, if only it was put back in their hands.</b>"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.24de4250-77cb-431e-ab21-0f6bd877f377"
msgid ""
"“Why did we have to give all of our data to all these people? It’s just because we couldn’t really do it ourselves,” Cheung said, adding that Zo’s answer is a service where people can gather their "
"data in one place, “like an infinite computer” that users can interrogate and answer questions they didn’t know how to ask before."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.24de4250-77cb-431e-ab21-0f6bd877f377"
msgid ""
"As the massive popularity of <a id=\"a1\">Spotify Wrapped</a> and similar features like Letterboxd’s ‘Year in Review’ has made clear, users are interested in accessing and parsing their data as a "
"means of quantifying and understanding themselves. However, this interest is colliding with a growing backlash to the AI boom and wariness about personal information being scraped for profit. Zo "
"Computer has positioned itself as an answer to these seemingly opposing desires, providing personal clouds while pledging not to use user data for AI training."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.24de4250-77cb-431e-ab21-0f6bd877f377"
msgid ""
"When asked why users should trust a young company with their information, Cheung cited portability: “It’s a raw computer, it’s just totally yours. You can leave at any time, and you can just take "
"all the data with you,” he said. Still, Zo’s no-training promise covers only Zo itself. When users ask its AI assistant a question, portions of their files go to whichever outside model provider "
"they’ve chosen, such as OpenAI, Anthropic, or Google, and those providers’ handling of the data is governed by their own terms. Users who want to keep everything in-house can instead point the "
"assistant to a self-hosted open-source model, and Cheung said Zo will soon let users rent GPU machines through the platform to run open-source models and keep everything “closed loop.”"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.52e53d5a-7f67-4ffb-9a92-a8f00ad4e4d3"
msgid ""
"The emergence of companies like Zo comes as a broader “de-Googling” movement has been mounting for some time. Users who are skeptical of Big Tech, advocating for more European digital sovereignty, "
"or who are simply tired of AI slop are increasingly migrating to other services, says Kyle Bradley, who in 2024 started creating guides to help people do just that. He said he first saw an uptick in"
" interest after Trump’s <b>second</b> inauguration, and started an online community called <a id=\"a1\">Purchase with Purpose</a> to help people find alternative services. The platform now has "
"30,000 members and his guides have upwards of 10 million views."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.52e53d5a-7f67-4ffb-9a92-a8f00ad4e4d3"
msgid ""
"“AI has people feeling that their data is now being used against them,” Bradley said. “It’s not just about data being collected generally—it’s about data being weaponized to take your job and profit"
" these companies further.”"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.52e53d5a-7f67-4ffb-9a92-a8f00ad4e4d3"
msgid ""
"The desire to flee Big Tech is not only ideological, but also related to a desire for a better user experience, Bradley noted. After Google integrated AI summaries at the top of searches on its "
"platform, alternative DuckDuckGo has seen user numbers <a id=\"a1\">spike</a> 33% <b>week-over-week in May of 2026</b>. While some users want full privacy and encryption, others are just tired of "
"AI-induced “<a id=\"a2\">enshittification</a>,” <b>or a slow erosion of quality across digital products</b>. Overall, Bradley said, the anti-AI sentiment is “very, very strong.” People are "
"increasingly demanding ownership over their data—or at the very least, transparency over how it is being used."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.52e53d5a-7f67-4ffb-9a92-a8f00ad4e4d3"
msgid ""
"“There's a lot of frustration around who's benefiting from the data, and whether they are actually being held liable for the data they are scraping and stealing,” he said. “It kind of feels like the"
" Wild West at the moment.”"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.52e53d5a-7f67-4ffb-9a92-a8f00ad4e4d3"
msgid ""
"While some people are responding to AI anxieties by going fully offline—downloading their data to <a id=\"a1\">hard drives</a> or relying on <a id=\"a2\">physical media</a>—others <b>worry these "
"kinds of retreats carry their own costs. Personal clouds like Zo and locally hosted systems can give individuals control over their own data, but AI systems are built and trained on information at "
"scale. When certain communities pull back, that loss is shared.</b>"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.0e52da40-ed24-46c9-9a4d-93b129ab865e"
msgid ""
"“People have watched their content get scraped, repackaged, and used in systems they had no say in and saw no benefit from,” said EM Lewis-Jong, founder of the Mozilla Data Collective (MDC). “When "
"that happens repeatedly, the rational response is to retreat—to pull content offline or seek out spaces where they retain control. That retreat is exactly what weakens the commons over time.”"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.0e52da40-ed24-46c9-9a4d-93b129ab865e"
msgid ""
"The result, Lewis-Jong said, is that AI reinforces the internet’s blind spots. “When AI systems are trained on data scraped from the internet indiscriminately, the outputs reflect the same biases as"
" the internet: prejudice, poor language coverage, and cultural erasure,” they said. “Huge swathes of the world's populations are simply missing from those datasets.”"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.0e52da40-ed24-46c9-9a4d-93b129ab865e"
msgid ""
"MDC, which launched in November 2025, is trying to build a system that fills those gaps, using data sourced with consent at the forefront. Contributors have uploaded cultural content including "
"Hazaragi literature from Afghanistan, oral histories in Mada from Cameroon, Romansh newspapers from Switzerland, and videos of Elders sharing Nigerian Ekpeye folktales. The platform now hosts 265 "
"vetted contributors, from podcasters, museums, and newspapers, to individual artists and even preachers. More than 28,000 companies, nonprofits, and researchers have downloaded datasets since MDC’s "
"launch, drawn to information that does not exist in mainstream channels and is sourced with consent and clear provenance."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.0e52da40-ed24-46c9-9a4d-93b129ab865e"
msgid ""
"Where many platforms rely on users’ trust and their ability to take data and leave if problems arise, MDC builds consent in from the ground up: contributors decide how their data is used and whether"
" to share it under an open license or to charge for it, with a 5% fee on paid downloads funding the platform."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.0e52da40-ed24-46c9-9a4d-93b129ab865e"
msgid "“We shouldn't be creating datasets without community input, context, or consent,” Lewis-Jong said. “We should be building with consent throughout the dataset supply chain.”"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.0e52da40-ed24-46c9-9a4d-93b129ab865e"
msgid ""
"Marcelle Chagas, a researcher and fellow at the Mozilla Foundation who contributed to MDC through her project Digital Territories, said she has seen firsthand what is at stake for communities left "
"out of these decisions. Her report was developed through fieldwork with Indigenous and Quilombola communities in Brazil, and found many people there had mixed feelings about AI. While more than half"
" of participants had heard about AI and 38% identified potential benefits—particularly for education, communication, and access to information—42% associated it with risks such as surveillance, "
"information control, and loss of autonomy."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.e8780d01-4298-4e68-af94-c189c11496b3.title"
msgid "Image by Logan Voss"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.e8780d01-4298-4e68-af94-c189c11496b3.image.alt_text"
msgid "logan-voss-etKJUV5BKpQ-unsplash"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.582c1ccd-d45d-4510-9ba9-b2e844b272d8"
msgid ""
"Part of that wariness came from a sense of being outside the institutions governing and creating these systems. Communities in the Global South, she said, feed their data, knowledge, and cultural "
"practices into these systems while “exercising little influence over how these resources are collected, interpreted, or reused.”"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.582c1ccd-d45d-4510-9ba9-b2e844b272d8"
msgid ""
"Chagas said the research underscored ways in which AI could be beneficial when it served local needs and was introduced by people the communities already trusted. Some participants said they had "
"used AI tools to better understand legal and technical information about territorial rights."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.582c1ccd-d45d-4510-9ba9-b2e844b272d8"
msgid ""
"“Trust was not placed in AI alone, but in processes of mediation, validation, and collective interpretation,” she said. “The technology functioned as a support tool rather than a replacement for "
"human judgment.”"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.582c1ccd-d45d-4510-9ba9-b2e844b272d8"
msgid ""
"Cheung sees a version of the same tension at the individual level: personal data can be genuinely useful to the people who generate it, as long as they decide where it goes. “I don’t think the "
"answer is to throw your phone in the ocean,” he said. “But importantly, you don’t want to send your sleep data to someone who’s gonna sell you, I don’t know, sleep pads.”"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.582c1ccd-d45d-4510-9ba9-b2e844b272d8"
msgid ""
"Lewis-Jong noted that the effects of MDC are ongoing and evolving quickly along with the fast-changing AI landscape. Having entered this research space in 2017, they said they are heartened to see "
"new organizations coming into the space every day."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.582c1ccd-d45d-4510-9ba9-b2e844b272d8"
msgid ""
"“What I'd point to is that norms are still being actively written,” Lewis-Jong said. “This is a moment where the standards are elastic and ours to shape. The question of whether consent and "
"sovereignty is treated as ‘friction’ or as a baseline right; that is being decided right now, and I think the momentum is moving toward consent becoming the expectation rather than the exception. "
"That's the change we want to accelerate.”"
msgstr ""

